1
00:00:00,084 --> 00:00:01,584
<i>Previously on</i> Reacher...

2
00:00:01,585 --> 00:00:03,086
[Foster] <i>No one knows
the three of you are here.</i>

3
00:00:03,087 --> 00:00:05,380
We are treating you
like enemy combatants on U.S. soil.

4
00:00:05,381 --> 00:00:06,798
We can do whatever we want.

5
00:00:06,799 --> 00:00:09,884
Where is the drive that Anna Merrick gave
to you on the train?

6
00:00:09,885 --> 00:00:13,054
[grunting]

7
00:00:13,055 --> 00:00:15,640
[dramatic music playing]

8
00:00:15,641 --> 00:00:17,434
- Get in!
- [Reacher] <i>Who are you?</i>

9
00:00:17,435 --> 00:00:20,562
Russell Plum, freelance journalist.

10
00:00:20,563 --> 00:00:22,230
- [Tamara] What is all this?
- [Russell] My research.

11
00:00:22,231 --> 00:00:24,315
I've been investigating the CIA for years.

12
00:00:24,316 --> 00:00:27,193
In the '90s, my mother met
a U.S. soldier named John.

13
00:00:27,194 --> 00:00:29,571
<i>Congressman John Sampson.</i>

14
00:00:29,572 --> 00:00:32,449
They had an affair, and you're basically
walking with the result of that union.

15
00:00:32,450 --> 00:00:34,451
My doctors say
I need a bone marrow transplant,

16
00:00:34,452 --> 00:00:36,161
but matches are hard to come by.

17
00:00:36,162 --> 00:00:38,413
You were in country
when my source was conceived.

18
00:00:38,414 --> 00:00:39,581
You think I had an affair?

19
00:00:39,582 --> 00:00:42,752
If my DNA's all it's gonna take
to get you to leave us alone...

20
00:00:43,794 --> 00:00:45,253
...here you go.

21
00:00:45,254 --> 00:00:46,672
[Tamara] Someone was looking
for the drive.

22
00:00:47,840 --> 00:00:50,842
- Your nephew?
- Yeah, I wish he'd call me back.

23
00:00:50,843 --> 00:00:52,427
Hey, who's that guy talking to Sampson?

24
00:00:52,428 --> 00:00:55,096
[paparazzo] That's Nolan Cahill,
Sampson's chief of staff.

25
00:00:55,097 --> 00:00:56,598
Remember when I said
there was a fourth witness

26
00:00:56,599 --> 00:00:59,477
on the train when Anna killed herself?
That's him.

27
00:01:10,279 --> 00:01:12,281
[siren wails in distance]

28
00:01:13,949 --> 00:01:15,951
[♪ Squeeze playing "Tempted"]

29
00:01:17,119 --> 00:01:18,703
[Russell] You're back.

30
00:01:18,704 --> 00:01:20,663
Van's downstairs.

31
00:01:20,664 --> 00:01:22,874
- Here.
- What's this?

32
00:01:22,875 --> 00:01:24,292
A cat.

33
00:01:24,293 --> 00:01:26,795
We need all the information
you can get on Nolan Cahill.

34
00:01:26,796 --> 00:01:28,838
He's John Sampson's chief of staff.

35
00:01:28,839 --> 00:01:30,131
Why? What's going on?

36
00:01:30,132 --> 00:01:31,842
My sister, uh...

37
00:01:33,427 --> 00:01:35,637
...Anna, she, um...

38
00:01:35,638 --> 00:01:38,014
The one you were talking about
in the van?

39
00:01:38,015 --> 00:01:40,225
Works for the Pentagon,
had some kind of jump drive?

40
00:01:40,226 --> 00:01:41,935
She shot herself
in the head in front of me

41
00:01:41,936 --> 00:01:43,520
two nights ago on the Broad Street Line.

42
00:01:43,521 --> 00:01:44,896
[cat mews]

43
00:01:44,897 --> 00:01:47,023
<i>♪ laundry on the hill ♪</i>

44
00:01:47,024 --> 00:01:49,400
<i>♪ billboards and the buildings ♪</i>

45
00:01:49,401 --> 00:01:52,403
<i>♪ Memories of it still keep calling ♪</i>

46
00:01:52,404 --> 00:01:53,780
A little tact?

47
00:01:53,781 --> 00:01:55,740
<i>♪ and calling...</i>

48
00:01:55,741 --> 00:01:57,700
Cahill was on the train
the night she died.

49
00:01:57,701 --> 00:01:58,993
We need to know why.

50
00:01:58,994 --> 00:02:00,870
A woman kills herself on the same train

51
00:02:00,871 --> 00:02:03,331
as the chief of staff
of a potential presidential candidate.

52
00:02:03,332 --> 00:02:05,792
The CIA starts kidnapping people over it.

53
00:02:05,793 --> 00:02:07,544
It-It's a tragedy,

54
00:02:07,545 --> 00:02:11,090
clearly, of course, but that's a story.

55
00:02:12,049 --> 00:02:13,675
Give me a beat,
I'll get into some records.

56
00:02:13,676 --> 00:02:17,387
<i>You got Ben. Leave a message, or live like
you're in the 21st century and text me.</i>

57
00:02:17,388 --> 00:02:18,346
<i>[beeps]</i>

58
00:02:18,347 --> 00:02:20,014
[female voice] <i>The mailbox is full</i>

59
00:02:20,015 --> 00:02:22,351
<i>- and cannot--</i>
- Fuck!

60
00:02:23,644 --> 00:02:25,436
<i>♪ another ♪</i>

61
00:02:25,437 --> 00:02:28,606
<i>♪ Tempted, but the truth is discovered...</i>

62
00:02:28,607 --> 00:02:30,025
I got it.

63
00:02:31,151 --> 00:02:32,278
I got it. Sorry.

64
00:02:33,362 --> 00:02:34,988
He's a liability.

65
00:02:34,989 --> 00:02:36,573
Inexperienced, all emotion.

66
00:02:36,574 --> 00:02:38,533
- He's a cop.
- From Mayberry.

67
00:02:38,534 --> 00:02:40,243
- Mayfield.
- Same thing.

68
00:02:40,244 --> 00:02:43,121
We don't know what we're up against.
We need all the help we can find.

69
00:02:43,122 --> 00:02:44,789
- He can't help us.
- How do you know?

70
00:02:44,790 --> 00:02:47,500
You ever have a sibling get killed
over some fucked-up illegal shit?

71
00:02:47,501 --> 00:02:49,127
Yes.

72
00:02:49,128 --> 00:02:50,670
- What?
- Bottom line?

73
00:02:50,671 --> 00:02:53,632
He doesn't pull it together,
I'll leave him on the side of the road.

74
00:02:54,633 --> 00:02:56,050
Who are you calling?

75
00:02:56,051 --> 00:02:59,178
Lila. Sampson's being straight
about not being her father.

76
00:02:59,179 --> 00:03:02,056
Jumped at the chance to provide DNA.

77
00:03:02,057 --> 00:03:04,183
I want to know why she lied to me
and what she really knows.

78
00:03:04,184 --> 00:03:06,270
Okay, found a lot.

79
00:03:07,897 --> 00:03:10,440
Cahill served
on the same Delta Team as Sampson.

80
00:03:10,441 --> 00:03:12,066
He was Sampson's commanding officer. Huh.

81
00:03:12,067 --> 00:03:14,402
He's got more medals than the Olympics.

82
00:03:14,403 --> 00:03:16,112
- Man like that knows a lot.
- [Jacob] Great.

83
00:03:16,113 --> 00:03:18,281
Now what are we doing here?
Let's go and confront him.

84
00:03:18,282 --> 00:03:20,575
- He won't talk.
- Then we make him talk.

85
00:03:20,576 --> 00:03:23,620
We can't pull a congressman's chief
of staff off the street and rough him up.

86
00:03:23,621 --> 00:03:25,747
That girl was sliced to pieces.

87
00:03:25,748 --> 00:03:27,874
I want my fuckin' nephew back!

88
00:03:27,875 --> 00:03:30,335
Jacob, we're not fighting you. It's just--

89
00:03:30,336 --> 00:03:32,211
- [alarm sounding]
- Someone's outside.

90
00:03:32,212 --> 00:03:33,796
[alarm stops]

91
00:03:33,797 --> 00:03:36,091
[dramatic, eerie music playing]

92
00:03:37,509 --> 00:03:39,469
[Reacher] It's the CIA backup squad
from the black site.

93
00:03:39,470 --> 00:03:42,513
- How'd they find us?
- Oh, shit.

94
00:03:42,514 --> 00:03:43,974
[Reacher] What'd you do?

95
00:03:44,975 --> 00:03:47,518
After you guys ditched me,
I may have posted something on 4Chan

96
00:03:47,519 --> 00:03:50,188
saying I found a thumb drive
on the Philly subway.

97
00:03:50,189 --> 00:03:54,192
It had Anna Merrick's name on the side
and military records on it.

98
00:03:54,193 --> 00:03:56,611
I asked if anyone had any intel,
maybe they should just reach out.

99
00:03:56,612 --> 00:03:57,695
What the fuck?

100
00:03:57,696 --> 00:03:59,656
You guys wouldn't tell me anything,
and you stole my van.

101
00:03:59,657 --> 00:04:01,616
All right? This story could give me
my career back.

102
00:04:01,617 --> 00:04:03,993
I figured I could flush out someone
who might know something.

103
00:04:03,994 --> 00:04:06,663
I used a VPN, I used an encryption
so they couldn't trace it.

104
00:04:06,664 --> 00:04:07,581
Looks like someone could.

105
00:04:08,749 --> 00:04:10,833
[Reacher] Maybe two someones.

106
00:04:10,834 --> 00:04:14,170
[Russell] Yeah, looks like
we got another car full of thugs.

107
00:04:14,171 --> 00:04:16,547
[Reacher] That one with the bruise was
at Lila's hotel yesterday.

108
00:04:16,548 --> 00:04:18,383
Who the hell are these guys?

109
00:04:18,384 --> 00:04:19,676
Doesn't matter
if they're private contractors,

110
00:04:19,677 --> 00:04:21,678
federal boys or the '75 Cincinnati Reds.

111
00:04:21,679 --> 00:04:22,971
If they're here, it's not good.

112
00:04:22,972 --> 00:04:25,348
Think the Big Red Machine's working
with the CIA as ghost ops?

113
00:04:25,349 --> 00:04:26,517
Maybe.

114
00:04:29,561 --> 00:04:31,229
I don't think they're working together.

115
00:04:31,230 --> 00:04:32,438
They're coming in.

116
00:04:32,439 --> 00:04:34,065
[powering down]

117
00:04:34,066 --> 00:04:35,900
Oh, shit. They cut the power.

118
00:04:35,901 --> 00:04:37,318
They'll use a standard protocol search,

119
00:04:37,319 --> 00:04:38,987
work their way up floor by floor
until they find us.

120
00:04:38,988 --> 00:04:40,488
[Russell] There's only one set of stairs.

121
00:04:40,489 --> 00:04:42,240
- We'll run straight into 'em.
- [Jacob] Is there any way off the roof?

122
00:04:42,241 --> 00:04:43,950
- Or a fire escape?
- No.

123
00:04:43,951 --> 00:04:46,327
- Where are your knives?
- I don't have any knives. I eat take-out.

124
00:04:46,328 --> 00:04:49,080
- You got any weapons?
- I have five cans of wasp spray.

125
00:04:49,081 --> 00:04:50,581
- Why?
- I have wasps.

126
00:04:50,582 --> 00:04:52,835
Get 'em.
Jacob, grab the fire extinguisher.

127
00:04:55,504 --> 00:04:58,339
- [mewing]
- Stay here.

128
00:04:58,340 --> 00:05:01,510
Use that to create cover so Tamara can
start shooting when she hears the signal.

129
00:05:03,721 --> 00:05:05,055
What's the signal?

130
00:05:06,098 --> 00:05:07,141
Screaming.

131
00:05:08,142 --> 00:05:10,144
[thunder rumbling]

132
00:05:21,155 --> 00:05:23,157
[tense, dramatic music playing]

133
00:05:30,914 --> 00:05:32,875
[Jacob] Come on, hurry. They're coming.

134
00:05:36,670 --> 00:05:39,005
[Reacher grunts]

135
00:05:39,006 --> 00:05:41,008
[panting]

136
00:05:50,809 --> 00:05:52,060
[grunts]

137
00:05:52,061 --> 00:05:54,897
[groaning]

138
00:06:06,658 --> 00:06:07,785
[man] Clear!

139
00:06:14,166 --> 00:06:15,708
- [Reacher grunts softly]
- [man 2] Clear.

140
00:06:15,709 --> 00:06:17,711
[groaning]

141
00:06:22,674 --> 00:06:25,635
- Keep it down.
- I can't find the fucking wasp spray.

142
00:06:25,636 --> 00:06:28,180
[strained groaning]

143
00:06:31,183 --> 00:06:32,518
[grunts]

144
00:06:38,023 --> 00:06:40,025
[panting]

145
00:06:43,987 --> 00:06:45,989
[slow, tense music playing]

146
00:06:52,955 --> 00:06:55,958
[footsteps approaching]

147
00:07:10,806 --> 00:07:12,808
[breathing heavily]

148
00:07:24,611 --> 00:07:26,195
- [grunting]
- [man yelling]

149
00:07:26,196 --> 00:07:27,572
Now!

150
00:07:27,573 --> 00:07:29,282
- [gunfire]
- [yelling]

151
00:07:29,283 --> 00:07:32,410
[gunfire continues]

152
00:07:32,411 --> 00:07:34,245
[yelling]

153
00:07:34,246 --> 00:07:35,580
- [gunshot]
- [grunts]

154
00:07:35,581 --> 00:07:39,293
[grunting]

155
00:07:45,174 --> 00:07:47,176
[panting]

156
00:07:50,929 --> 00:07:53,265
[man panting]

157
00:08:01,190 --> 00:08:03,233
Still alive? Good.

158
00:08:04,568 --> 00:08:05,777
[grunting]

159
00:08:08,155 --> 00:08:10,157
[head continues clanging on steps]

160
00:08:14,244 --> 00:08:15,828
- [grunts]
- Who do you work for?

161
00:08:15,829 --> 00:08:18,122
- I need a doctor.
- You want a doctor?

162
00:08:18,123 --> 00:08:20,208
- I want to know who hired you.
- Please.

163
00:08:20,209 --> 00:08:22,543
- Hey, tell me where Ben is right now!
- Hey. Get him out of here!

164
00:08:22,544 --> 00:08:25,087
- [grunts]
- You have to stay calm.

165
00:08:25,088 --> 00:08:26,964
Stay calm. He knows where Ben is.

166
00:08:26,965 --> 00:08:28,467
But he can't say shit if you kill him.

167
00:08:29,468 --> 00:08:31,720
We need that man alive
if we're gonna get intel.

168
00:08:34,306 --> 00:08:37,559
Uh... that's not gonna happen.

169
00:08:39,478 --> 00:08:42,356
- You're robbing him?
- We need to figure out who these guys are.

170
00:08:44,024 --> 00:08:46,275
Their guns could be traced back
to murdered CIA.

171
00:08:46,276 --> 00:08:48,778
- Oh, shit.
- What is it?

172
00:08:48,779 --> 00:08:52,531
Two addresses. One's this place,
one's hopefully not what I think it is.

173
00:08:52,532 --> 00:08:54,951
[Tamara] All right, we got to move, guys.

174
00:08:54,952 --> 00:08:56,327
[line ringing]

175
00:08:56,328 --> 00:08:57,870
Lila, it's Reacher.

176
00:08:57,871 --> 00:08:59,872
Your pal from the hotel yesterday
paid another visit.

177
00:08:59,873 --> 00:09:02,083
He's not a problem anymore,
but he had the address

178
00:09:02,084 --> 00:09:03,834
to the Rittenhouse Hotel on him.

179
00:09:03,835 --> 00:09:06,003
If that's where you are, leave now.

180
00:09:06,004 --> 00:09:07,255
I'll find you.

181
00:09:07,256 --> 00:09:08,673
[♪ Urban Dance Squad sings
"A Deeper Shade of Soul"]

182
00:09:08,674 --> 00:09:10,549
<i>♪ A deeper shade of soul, yeah ♪</i>

183
00:09:10,550 --> 00:09:14,804
- <i>♪ A deeper shade of soul ♪</i>
- <i>♪ Took a long time...</i>

184
00:09:14,805 --> 00:09:17,098
- [panting loudly]
- Hey?

185
00:09:17,099 --> 00:09:18,599
- What?
- You good?

186
00:09:18,600 --> 00:09:21,394
Yeah, man. I...
I just got to get out of D.C.

187
00:09:21,395 --> 00:09:23,145
I mean, you have no idea
what this city can be like

188
00:09:23,146 --> 00:09:24,647
when they have you in their crosshairs.

189
00:09:24,648 --> 00:09:27,108
They killed my career.
Now they're gonna kill me.

190
00:09:27,109 --> 00:09:29,278
Can we please just go any faster?

191
00:09:30,279 --> 00:09:32,363
We need to make a stop in town first.

192
00:09:32,364 --> 00:09:33,906
- Wha...?
- What? Are you kidding me?

193
00:09:33,907 --> 00:09:35,366
We're on the run, and we need cash.

194
00:09:35,367 --> 00:09:37,285
Got to hit an ATM while we're in the city.

195
00:09:37,286 --> 00:09:38,828
The people that want us dead
know we're in D.C.

196
00:09:38,829 --> 00:09:41,205
By the time they get a bead on the ATM,
we're long gone.

197
00:09:41,206 --> 00:09:42,957
Yeah, man. Might be following us already.

198
00:09:42,958 --> 00:09:45,418
Before they came up to my loft,
they saw the van

199
00:09:45,419 --> 00:09:47,586
in the garage, they probably called it in
to their bosses.

200
00:09:47,587 --> 00:09:49,005
Our vehicle's been compromised.

201
00:09:49,006 --> 00:09:50,506
You got another skin
under the Plumber sign?

202
00:09:50,507 --> 00:09:52,842
Yeah, but they have the make and model.
We got to get a new ride.

203
00:09:52,843 --> 00:09:54,135
We're wanted by the CIA.

204
00:09:54,136 --> 00:09:56,847
We can't exactly go to Hertz
and give 'em our licenses.

205
00:09:57,848 --> 00:09:58,849
I know a place.

206
00:09:59,850 --> 00:10:01,184
We can be there in less than two hours.

207
00:10:02,185 --> 00:10:05,188
{\an8}[♪ A.S.M. featuring Sadat X plays
"Certified Organic"]

208
00:10:10,861 --> 00:10:13,195
<i>♪ You've not heard of us,
this is certified organic</i>...

209
00:10:13,196 --> 00:10:14,447
[man] Yo, Cyrus.

210
00:10:14,448 --> 00:10:15,990
<i>♪ On its way, don't panic...</i>

211
00:10:15,991 --> 00:10:18,826
[tool whirring]

212
00:10:18,827 --> 00:10:21,996
Ah, fuck this. Nah, nah, nah, nah.

213
00:10:21,997 --> 00:10:23,914
Me and Docherty cut a deal.

214
00:10:23,915 --> 00:10:27,460
I get you dirt on those boys
from Norristown, you leave me alone.

215
00:10:27,461 --> 00:10:29,211
Docherty made that deal, not me.

216
00:10:29,212 --> 00:10:30,338
I don't make deals with punks.

217
00:10:30,339 --> 00:10:32,882
- Then what you doing here?
- I need to make a deal with some punks.

218
00:10:32,883 --> 00:10:34,133
And who are your white boys?

219
00:10:34,134 --> 00:10:36,510
[Tamara] My backup dancers.
Want to trade my Windstar

220
00:10:36,511 --> 00:10:37,762
for your Highlander?

221
00:10:37,763 --> 00:10:41,515
[laughs] That's money. That's bullshit.

222
00:10:41,516 --> 00:10:43,726
We both know that SUV's stolen.

223
00:10:43,727 --> 00:10:45,686
So, if you want,

224
00:10:45,687 --> 00:10:48,397
we can continue this conversation
at the District while I run the VIN.

225
00:10:48,398 --> 00:10:51,192
That is, if it isn't already filed off.

226
00:10:51,193 --> 00:10:54,071
If you were gonna bust us,
you'd have done it by now.

227
00:10:55,030 --> 00:10:56,530
You are absolutely right.

228
00:10:56,531 --> 00:10:59,617
Guess we'll have to figure out
another way to negotiate.

229
00:10:59,618 --> 00:11:01,202
<i>♪ I may not be the best alive,
but I count ♪</i>

230
00:11:01,203 --> 00:11:02,829
<i>♪ No matter the amount
of the trials I face</i>...

231
00:11:05,332 --> 00:11:06,666
[Cyrus] What are you doing?

232
00:11:07,626 --> 00:11:09,418
Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa.

233
00:11:09,419 --> 00:11:12,463
Yo, that's 50 racks right there!
What you doing?

234
00:11:12,464 --> 00:11:14,632
What is she doing?

235
00:11:14,633 --> 00:11:16,133
Getting his attention.

236
00:11:16,134 --> 00:11:18,219
- Hey, yo--
- Let's try this again.

237
00:11:18,220 --> 00:11:19,804
My Windstar for your Highlander.

238
00:11:19,805 --> 00:11:22,890
'Cause in a few seconds, our only option
is to run our asses out of here.

239
00:11:22,891 --> 00:11:26,727
Shit. All right. All right.
All right, just put it out. Put it out.

240
00:11:26,728 --> 00:11:29,397
And throw in some plates
from a car that's properly registered.

241
00:11:29,398 --> 00:11:30,690
That Jetta?

242
00:11:31,775 --> 00:11:33,943
Nah, nah, nah.
That's my sister's car right there.

243
00:11:33,944 --> 00:11:36,113
Rayona'll kill me.
You know she's crazy, right?

244
00:11:36,947 --> 00:11:39,115
This rag is burning up pretty quickly.

245
00:11:39,116 --> 00:11:41,867
[tense, dramatic music playing]

246
00:11:41,868 --> 00:11:44,662
Okay. Okay. You know what?

247
00:11:44,663 --> 00:11:46,622
I think I can make it work with Ray Ray.

248
00:11:46,623 --> 00:11:49,166
I can figure it out.
Just please put it out. Put it out!

249
00:11:49,167 --> 00:11:51,169
[engine revving]

250
00:11:56,925 --> 00:11:58,926
[purring]

251
00:11:58,927 --> 00:12:00,970
- I'm-a call you Pancake.
- [mews]

252
00:12:00,971 --> 00:12:05,474
- [♪ The Coasters sing "Down in Mexico"]
<i>♪ Ah, ah ♪</i>

253
00:12:05,475 --> 00:12:08,477
<i>♪ Down in the Mexicali ♪</i>

254
00:12:08,478 --> 00:12:09,854
<i>♪ Bop, doo-wop ♪</i>

255
00:12:09,855 --> 00:12:11,647
<i>♪ There's a crazy little place</i>...

256
00:12:11,648 --> 00:12:13,357
I liked that cat.

257
00:12:13,358 --> 00:12:14,775
- Ray Ray's all right.
- [phone ringing]

258
00:12:14,776 --> 00:12:16,319
Kitty's got a good home.

259
00:12:19,573 --> 00:12:22,158
[phone rings over speaker]

260
00:12:22,159 --> 00:12:23,659
Lila.

261
00:12:23,660 --> 00:12:25,369
[Lila] <i>Hey, I got your message.</i>

262
00:12:25,370 --> 00:12:27,746
Moved my family
to a new hotel immediately.

263
00:12:27,747 --> 00:12:30,791
We're settling in,
we're just a little freaked out.

264
00:12:30,792 --> 00:12:33,502
We need to talk. Where can you meet?

265
00:12:33,503 --> 00:12:34,712
<i>The park again?</i>

266
00:12:34,713 --> 00:12:36,046
We need someplace inside

267
00:12:36,047 --> 00:12:38,757
where the people looking for us
can't just see us driving by.

268
00:12:38,758 --> 00:12:41,552
A place that's crowded,
but quiet enough we can talk.

269
00:12:41,553 --> 00:12:43,053
<i>No loud bars.</i>

270
00:12:43,054 --> 00:12:45,931
I passed a bowling alley on the way here.

271
00:12:45,932 --> 00:12:47,600
<i>Playtime Bowl on 2nd.</i>

272
00:12:47,601 --> 00:12:50,186
Perfect. Head there now.
I'll see you soon.

273
00:12:50,187 --> 00:12:51,480
<i>Reacher?</i>

274
00:12:52,272 --> 00:12:53,689
[sighs]

275
00:12:53,690 --> 00:12:55,066
Thank you.

276
00:12:57,402 --> 00:13:00,529
I need to get some info
from Lila to confront Cahill with.

277
00:13:00,530 --> 00:13:02,865
He's a powerful guy with D.C. connections.

278
00:13:02,866 --> 00:13:05,201
I can't just show up
and try to intimidate him.

279
00:13:05,202 --> 00:13:07,204
Where do you want me
to drop you three off?

280
00:13:08,288 --> 00:13:09,538
You're going alone?

281
00:13:09,539 --> 00:13:12,082
What if more mercs are following Lila?
You'll need backup.

282
00:13:12,083 --> 00:13:13,667
Didn't need backup at the hotel.

283
00:13:13,668 --> 00:13:15,336
I was with you.

284
00:13:15,337 --> 00:13:16,795
Yeah, out cold in the hall.

285
00:13:16,796 --> 00:13:19,924
Odds are slim they'll find one woman
in this city that fast.

286
00:13:19,925 --> 00:13:21,885
Hopefully. Lila stands out.

287
00:13:22,969 --> 00:13:24,261
How so?

288
00:13:24,262 --> 00:13:25,972
She's what the kids today
call a smoke show.

289
00:13:27,516 --> 00:13:29,433
That why you want to meet her alone?

290
00:13:29,434 --> 00:13:31,519
I caught her in a lie.

291
00:13:31,520 --> 00:13:34,188
Can't just show up with two cops.
She's not gonna open her mouth.

292
00:13:34,189 --> 00:13:35,815
I have to go alone.

293
00:13:37,317 --> 00:13:38,777
Mm-hmm.

294
00:13:39,694 --> 00:13:42,071
Drop us off at the District for my shift.

295
00:13:42,072 --> 00:13:43,739
I'll check in with Docherty,
see what he found

296
00:13:43,740 --> 00:13:46,992
while you... probe Lila.

297
00:13:46,993 --> 00:13:49,203
<i>♪ I didn't know just what to expect ♪</i>

298
00:13:49,204 --> 00:13:50,538
- Hmm.
- <i>♪ in Mexico ♪</i>

299
00:13:50,539 --> 00:13:53,541
<i>♪ She threw her arms around my neck ♪</i>

300
00:13:53,542 --> 00:13:54,875
<i>♪ in Mexico ♪</i>

301
00:13:54,876 --> 00:13:57,253
<i>♪ We started dancing
all around the floor ♪</i>

302
00:13:57,254 --> 00:13:58,837
<i>♪ in Mexico ♪</i>

303
00:13:58,838 --> 00:14:02,258
<i>♪ and then she did a dance
I never saw before ♪</i>

304
00:14:02,259 --> 00:14:04,261
- [phones ringing]
- [indistinct chatter]

305
00:14:05,845 --> 00:14:07,555
[Docherty] Holy shit.

306
00:14:07,556 --> 00:14:11,058
It's Halley's comet,
coming by once every hundred years.

307
00:14:11,059 --> 00:14:13,394
It's 75, dickhead.
Where are we on the Merrick case?

308
00:14:13,395 --> 00:14:15,229
Look, fuck you. You were MIA again.

309
00:14:15,230 --> 00:14:17,064
It's becoming a habit.
I'm done helping you.

310
00:14:17,065 --> 00:14:19,441
I don't have time for this shit, man.
We're working something here.

311
00:14:19,442 --> 00:14:21,527
We? Who? You and the Fuck-It-Up Gang?

312
00:14:21,528 --> 00:14:24,905
The Feds took the case.
It's not our hot dog cart any more, Slick.

313
00:14:24,906 --> 00:14:27,449
That dead OnlyFans girl was found
in Philadelphia.

314
00:14:27,450 --> 00:14:29,702
That's our jurisdiction.
So what do we got?

315
00:14:29,703 --> 00:14:31,203
Or should I not give a shit
about homicides

316
00:14:31,204 --> 00:14:32,621
that happen in my own city anymore?

317
00:14:32,622 --> 00:14:34,665
- You're not a homicide detective.
- Neither are you.

318
00:14:34,666 --> 00:14:35,874
[chuckles]

319
00:14:35,875 --> 00:14:37,419
What are you smiling at, Pee Wee?

320
00:14:38,503 --> 00:14:42,298
Look, whoever hired that girl, that escort
or whatever, they covered their tracks.

321
00:14:42,299 --> 00:14:44,550
VPN, offshore accounts. Untraceable.

322
00:14:44,551 --> 00:14:45,801
What about the ME?

323
00:14:45,802 --> 00:14:47,261
It's gonna be a few days on the report.

324
00:14:47,262 --> 00:14:48,346
Did you expedite it?

325
00:14:49,306 --> 00:14:51,015
- Yeah.
- And the guy in the library?

326
00:14:51,016 --> 00:14:53,684
The fucking tip line? Was that you?

327
00:14:53,685 --> 00:14:55,936
It doesn't matter who called it in.
What do we got on him?

328
00:14:55,937 --> 00:15:00,065
[exhales] His prints didn't match
anything in the girl's room.

329
00:15:00,066 --> 00:15:02,318
- And?
- You know what, guys?

330
00:15:02,319 --> 00:15:04,278
There's Tastykakes in the kitchen.

331
00:15:04,279 --> 00:15:06,615
- Cool. I could eat.
- I'm not hungry.

332
00:15:08,074 --> 00:15:10,952
Then grab some coffee. I got this.

333
00:15:15,248 --> 00:15:17,166
Ah, now what the hell are you doing?

334
00:15:17,167 --> 00:15:20,127
You already rat-fucked your career,
now you want to do it to mine?

335
00:15:20,128 --> 00:15:21,295
Got nothing to do with you.

336
00:15:21,296 --> 00:15:23,964
You know damn well
your shit splatters on me.

337
00:15:23,965 --> 00:15:26,717
I got a 40-year vest coming.
I ain't gonna let you shit it up.

338
00:15:26,718 --> 00:15:28,969
Big D, we found the Self Check-Out's car.

339
00:15:28,970 --> 00:15:31,305
- Near the subway stop?
- No.

340
00:15:31,306 --> 00:15:35,434
Merrick got on at Tasker-Morris.
Car was picked up at 6th and Carpenter.

341
00:15:35,435 --> 00:15:37,686
RPC's babysitting it.
You want to feed the Feds?

342
00:15:37,687 --> 00:15:39,104
It was your case.

343
00:15:39,105 --> 00:15:41,274
You tell 'em. I don't care.

344
00:15:43,443 --> 00:15:44,861
Copy that.

345
00:15:50,867 --> 00:15:53,577
- It's not our case anymore.
- Anna Merrick isn't.

346
00:15:53,578 --> 00:15:55,621
No one said
I couldn't look into a stolen car.

347
00:15:55,622 --> 00:15:57,915
Could be a stolen car ring here in Philly.

348
00:15:57,916 --> 00:15:59,416
You've been phoning it in for years.

349
00:15:59,417 --> 00:16:01,252
Now, all of a sudden,
you want to be a cop again?

350
00:16:02,170 --> 00:16:06,049
Have you forgotten what happened
the last time you couldn't let a case go?

351
00:16:09,260 --> 00:16:11,846
I'm getting Tastykakes.

352
00:16:13,515 --> 00:16:16,433
A pain in my ass.

353
00:16:16,434 --> 00:16:18,478
We got the car.

354
00:16:19,688 --> 00:16:22,356
- [♪ Eddie Floyd sings "Knock on Wood"]
- <i>♪ I don't want to lose ♪</i>

355
00:16:22,357 --> 00:16:24,234
<i>♪ this good thing ♪</i>

356
00:16:26,403 --> 00:16:28,113
<i>♪ that I've got ♪</i>

357
00:16:28,822 --> 00:16:30,490
<i>♪ If I do ♪</i>

358
00:16:31,366 --> 00:16:33,826
<i>♪ I would surely...</i>

359
00:16:33,827 --> 00:16:36,286
Reacher, thank you again for warning us.

360
00:16:36,287 --> 00:16:38,623
It's the last thing I'll do for you
if you don't tell me the truth.

361
00:16:40,208 --> 00:16:42,084
You lied about Sampson.

362
00:16:42,085 --> 00:16:44,379
I don't believe he's your father,
and neither do you.

363
00:16:50,844 --> 00:16:52,344
Being honest is the least you can do,

364
00:16:52,345 --> 00:16:55,140
considering I confronted
a U.S. congressman with your bullshit.

365
00:17:01,938 --> 00:17:03,814
You're right.

366
00:17:03,815 --> 00:17:05,774
I wasn't entirely truthful.

367
00:17:05,775 --> 00:17:07,401
I'm a journalist.

368
00:17:07,402 --> 00:17:10,739
Amisha is not my mother,
and these two are hired protection.

369
00:17:11,823 --> 00:17:13,198
Protecting you from what?

370
00:17:13,199 --> 00:17:16,285
I'm here to expose the secrets of
the most powerful government on earth.

371
00:17:16,286 --> 00:17:17,536
And the cancer?

372
00:17:17,537 --> 00:17:20,205
Made the bruises myself
so I could get to Sampson.

373
00:17:20,206 --> 00:17:21,790
So you could lie to him, too?

374
00:17:21,791 --> 00:17:25,002
Look, I'm truly sorry, okay?

375
00:17:25,003 --> 00:17:27,130
But you could've had information
we needed.

376
00:17:28,298 --> 00:17:29,965
And I was scared.

377
00:17:29,966 --> 00:17:33,051
A woman died. Men came after me.

378
00:17:33,052 --> 00:17:35,138
Tell me the real story.

379
00:17:39,434 --> 00:17:40,894
Fries are communal.

380
00:17:41,644 --> 00:17:42,728
Go on.

381
00:17:42,729 --> 00:17:46,607
Amisha and Sampson didn't have an affair,
but they are connected in another way.

382
00:17:46,608 --> 00:17:49,860
When Amisha and her husband
were still students in Indonesia,

383
00:17:49,861 --> 00:17:52,362
they were concerned
with the growing influence of the military

384
00:17:52,363 --> 00:17:54,323
under a man named General Putra.

385
00:17:54,324 --> 00:17:56,617
So, they had rallies.

386
00:17:56,618 --> 00:17:59,912
And soon after that, the whole
entire group was illegally incarcerated.

387
00:17:59,913 --> 00:18:02,206
Putra committed atrocities.

388
00:18:02,207 --> 00:18:04,500
Amisha listened
as her husband was brutally beaten

389
00:18:04,501 --> 00:18:06,585
and killed in a cell next to hers.

390
00:18:06,586 --> 00:18:08,837
And then she was beaten
within an inch of her own life.

391
00:18:08,838 --> 00:18:13,091
Putra and his soldiers were trained
by an American Delta Force specialist.

392
00:18:13,092 --> 00:18:14,259
John Sampson.

393
00:18:14,260 --> 00:18:16,637
Yeah, and that's why he was in Indonesia
in the first place.

394
00:18:16,638 --> 00:18:19,348
To teach Putra's soldiers
advanced military tactics.

395
00:18:19,349 --> 00:18:23,268
The very tactics that were used against
the civilians Putra tortured and killed.

396
00:18:23,269 --> 00:18:24,812
What about Nolan Cahill?

397
00:18:24,813 --> 00:18:28,273
He was... he was Sampson's
commanding officer in Indonesia. Why?

398
00:18:28,274 --> 00:18:30,067
He was on the train the night Anna died.

399
00:18:30,068 --> 00:18:32,111
♪ ♪

400
00:18:35,782 --> 00:18:39,076
He must've been there to stop Anna
from delivering the drive to me,

401
00:18:39,077 --> 00:18:41,078
getting the story to me.

402
00:18:41,079 --> 00:18:44,457
America wants to scrub its involvement
clean, but the public deserves to know.

403
00:18:45,458 --> 00:18:46,792
After sitting with Amisha,

404
00:18:46,793 --> 00:18:49,753
listening to her story,
looking in her eyes, at her face,

405
00:18:49,754 --> 00:18:53,423
seeing what Putra did to her,
Anna Merrick agreed to help.

406
00:18:53,424 --> 00:18:54,967
And that's why
she was bringing me that drive,

407
00:18:54,968 --> 00:18:56,802
'cause she appreciated what's at stake.

408
00:18:56,803 --> 00:19:00,056
Bet she appreciated stealing
government records is a federal crime.

409
00:19:01,140 --> 00:19:03,934
Anna chose to work for the Pentagon
'cause she's a patriot.

410
00:19:03,935 --> 00:19:06,603
And she, like I,

411
00:19:06,604 --> 00:19:09,356
believed a man like Sampson,
a man with his morals,

412
00:19:09,357 --> 00:19:11,693
cannot take the White House.

413
00:19:12,694 --> 00:19:14,320
I have to get this story out.

414
00:19:20,034 --> 00:19:21,244
Please.

415
00:19:22,245 --> 00:19:23,788
I need your help.

416
00:19:25,707 --> 00:19:30,627
If it's not for me, for Amisha,
her husband and all the people killed.

417
00:19:30,628 --> 00:19:32,796
For Anna.

418
00:19:32,797 --> 00:19:35,758
That drive has the proof
connecting Sampson to all of this.

419
00:19:38,386 --> 00:19:40,512
I'll find the drive.

420
00:19:40,513 --> 00:19:42,598
When I see what's on it,

421
00:19:42,599 --> 00:19:44,642
I'll decide what to do with it.

422
00:19:45,894 --> 00:19:47,811
That's all I can ask. Thank you.

423
00:19:47,812 --> 00:19:50,440
[dramatic, suspenseful music playing]

424
00:19:55,445 --> 00:19:57,195
Don't thank me yet.

425
00:19:57,196 --> 00:19:58,780
Looks like the Big Red Machine found us.

426
00:19:58,781 --> 00:20:00,366
- How?
- You were followed.

427
00:20:01,284 --> 00:20:02,910
Might want to hire some new security.

428
00:20:02,911 --> 00:20:04,787
What are you gonna do?

429
00:20:07,415 --> 00:20:08,958
Make 'em regret walking in here.

430
00:20:10,376 --> 00:20:11,336
[man] Perhaps not.

431
00:20:14,505 --> 00:20:16,507
Guns on the table, or the girl dies.

432
00:20:18,092 --> 00:20:19,719
Now.

433
00:20:23,348 --> 00:20:25,599
Get up. We're taking a walk.

434
00:20:25,600 --> 00:20:27,559
Let's go. Sit down.

435
00:20:27,560 --> 00:20:29,270
You, sit down.

436
00:20:31,606 --> 00:20:36,693
[DJ over speaker] <i>It's time
for Midnight Cosmic Bowl!</i>

437
00:20:36,694 --> 00:20:39,446
<i>All strikes get a free pitcher
of Rolling Rock,</i>

438
00:20:39,447 --> 00:20:43,492
<i>and we got buy-one-get-one apps!</i>

439
00:20:43,493 --> 00:20:45,577
- [applause and cheering]
- [airhorn sounding]

440
00:20:45,578 --> 00:20:47,580
[♪ KC and the Sunshine band singing
"Boogie Shoes"]

441
00:20:52,919 --> 00:20:54,086
- Get them out of here.
- [screaming]

442
00:20:54,087 --> 00:20:58,799
<i>♪ I can't wait till I see you again</i>...

443
00:20:58,800 --> 00:21:01,177
[grunting]

444
00:21:03,096 --> 00:21:08,100
<i>♪ I want to on put on my, my, my, my,
my boogie shoes ♪</i>

445
00:21:08,101 --> 00:21:11,061
<i>♪ Just to boogie with you, yeah ♪</i>

446
00:21:11,062 --> 00:21:14,606
<i>♪ I want to put on my, my, my, my,
my boogie shoes...</i>

447
00:21:14,607 --> 00:21:16,900
[people screaming]

448
00:21:16,901 --> 00:21:19,111
[grunting]

449
00:21:19,112 --> 00:21:23,199
<i>♪ I want to do it till the sun comes up...</i>

450
00:21:24,283 --> 00:21:25,909
Come on.

451
00:21:25,910 --> 00:21:27,369
<i>♪ Oh, yeah ♪</i>

452
00:21:27,370 --> 00:21:31,039
<i>♪ I want to do it
till I can't get enough ♪</i>

453
00:21:31,040 --> 00:21:34,710
<i>♪ Yeah, yeah, uh-huh...</i>

454
00:21:34,711 --> 00:21:36,837
<i>♪ I want to put on...</i>

455
00:21:36,838 --> 00:21:40,550
[yelling]

456
00:21:42,510 --> 00:21:47,306
<i>♪ I want to put on my, my, my, my
my boogie shoes ♪</i>

457
00:21:48,641 --> 00:21:52,019
♪ ♪

458
00:21:52,020 --> 00:21:54,522
[grunting]

459
00:22:04,699 --> 00:22:07,118
- [panting]
- [wood clatters on ground]

460
00:22:08,953 --> 00:22:10,537
I told you to get out of here.

461
00:22:10,538 --> 00:22:12,372
The guards took Amisha to the hotel.

462
00:22:12,373 --> 00:22:14,083
I couldn't just leave you behind.

463
00:22:15,835 --> 00:22:17,335
Thank you.

464
00:22:17,336 --> 00:22:19,379
[grunts, exhales]

465
00:22:19,380 --> 00:22:21,339
[sirens wailing]

466
00:22:21,340 --> 00:22:24,342
Ah, we got to go.

467
00:22:24,343 --> 00:22:26,511
If I get caught in the system,
the CIA will find me.

468
00:22:26,512 --> 00:22:27,972
CIA?

469
00:22:29,015 --> 00:22:31,350
[indistinct radio chatter]

470
00:22:34,729 --> 00:22:36,147
[officer] Let 'em through.

471
00:22:51,788 --> 00:22:53,790
[insects trilling]

472
00:22:57,168 --> 00:22:59,711
- This is it?
- Yeah.

473
00:22:59,712 --> 00:23:02,840
Any idea why she'd ditch
a perfectly good car, take the subway?

474
00:23:03,883 --> 00:23:05,967
- None.
- Whoever took it

475
00:23:05,968 --> 00:23:07,636
knew exactly what they were doing.

476
00:23:07,637 --> 00:23:11,348
Take it to a secluded spot,
toss the hell out of it.

477
00:23:11,349 --> 00:23:13,141
Like Anna's house.

478
00:23:13,142 --> 00:23:15,018
CIA again?

479
00:23:15,019 --> 00:23:16,020
Maybe.

480
00:23:17,230 --> 00:23:19,981
Had to know
local cops were looking for this car.

481
00:23:19,982 --> 00:23:22,318
Didn't want to get caught
with it next to the subway.

482
00:23:23,277 --> 00:23:25,320
{\an8}Damn. Look at this shit.

483
00:23:25,321 --> 00:23:26,780
{\an8}[tense music playing]

484
00:23:26,781 --> 00:23:29,908
{\an8}Glove box forced open, seats shredded.

485
00:23:29,909 --> 00:23:31,369
Look at this mail.

486
00:23:33,788 --> 00:23:35,247
Credit card offer.

487
00:23:35,248 --> 00:23:37,499
"Act fast, get 5,000 extra points."

488
00:23:37,500 --> 00:23:40,210
If this was street hoods,
they would have taken this,

489
00:23:40,211 --> 00:23:41,628
run a little fraud.

490
00:23:41,629 --> 00:23:43,713
This was definitely
people looking for something.

491
00:23:43,714 --> 00:23:46,091
- The drive.
- [Tamara] Yeah.

492
00:23:46,092 --> 00:23:47,969
[slow, suspenseful music playing]

493
00:23:53,933 --> 00:23:55,601
Bear Creek pass.

494
00:23:56,936 --> 00:23:59,689
Never cleaned out her trunk
from last winter.

495
00:24:00,982 --> 00:24:03,191
So her winter jacket
would have been here, too.

496
00:24:03,192 --> 00:24:05,485
And you can't make a guess
as to why she would put it on

497
00:24:05,486 --> 00:24:06,821
and get on a hot subway?

498
00:24:08,322 --> 00:24:09,824
No idea.

499
00:24:11,701 --> 00:24:14,369
Well, looks like she just happened
to still have this stuff in the back.

500
00:24:14,370 --> 00:24:17,080
So if Anna left the house
without a coat,

501
00:24:17,081 --> 00:24:19,082
wearing one wasn't part of her plan.

502
00:24:19,083 --> 00:24:21,376
It became part of the plan

503
00:24:21,377 --> 00:24:23,128
after she got in the car.

504
00:24:23,129 --> 00:24:25,089
None of this is logical.

505
00:24:26,507 --> 00:24:27,800
Yeah.

506
00:24:31,387 --> 00:24:33,471
[melancholy music playing]

507
00:24:33,472 --> 00:24:35,016
You okay?

508
00:24:38,728 --> 00:24:40,438
I was supposed to go with her and Ben...

509
00:24:41,772 --> 00:24:42,899
...to Bear Creek...

510
00:24:44,150 --> 00:24:47,819
...but I canceled last minute
so I could...

511
00:24:47,820 --> 00:24:49,905
[laughs]

512
00:24:49,906 --> 00:24:52,575
...reinsulate my fucking garage.

513
00:24:55,286 --> 00:24:57,830
I am never gonna get
to do anything with them again.

514
00:25:00,833 --> 00:25:02,918
At least my garage is...

515
00:25:02,919 --> 00:25:04,252
warm, right?

516
00:25:04,253 --> 00:25:05,379
Hey.

517
00:25:06,547 --> 00:25:09,592
You know what kind of people put themself
through hell after a loved one dies?

518
00:25:10,927 --> 00:25:12,928
Like, shoulda, I-I coulda,

519
00:25:12,929 --> 00:25:14,347
I wish I had?

520
00:25:17,099 --> 00:25:19,185
Good people.

521
00:25:20,353 --> 00:25:22,355
But it doesn't help you...

522
00:25:24,398 --> 00:25:26,525
...and it's not gonna help finding
who did all this.

523
00:25:30,404 --> 00:25:31,948
So, you still with me?

524
00:25:32,949 --> 00:25:35,159
Head in the game?

525
00:25:40,414 --> 00:25:41,915
[sighs]

526
00:25:41,916 --> 00:25:43,416
All right.

527
00:25:43,417 --> 00:25:44,668
[Russell] No GPS.

528
00:25:44,669 --> 00:25:46,503
Phone mount on dash.

529
00:25:46,504 --> 00:25:49,090
Probably used her cell to navigate.

530
00:25:50,174 --> 00:25:54,052
There was no phone found on Anna at the
subway station, and nothing on the train.

531
00:25:54,053 --> 00:25:55,762
Well, whoever searched must have taken it.

532
00:25:55,763 --> 00:25:56,973
[Jacob] Move.

533
00:26:01,686 --> 00:26:03,812
It's a work badge.

534
00:26:03,813 --> 00:26:06,189
We found Anna's work badge at her house.

535
00:26:06,190 --> 00:26:08,858
Whole point of those things is security,
getting onto federal property.

536
00:26:08,859 --> 00:26:10,361
They don't give out extras.

537
00:26:11,445 --> 00:26:14,990
[inhales] Anna ever mention a second job
or working someplace new?

538
00:26:14,991 --> 00:26:17,325
- No. Never.
- [suspenseful music playing]

539
00:26:17,326 --> 00:26:19,036
[garbled radio transmission]

540
00:26:21,497 --> 00:26:24,500
Shit. Feds. We got to go.

541
00:26:26,377 --> 00:26:29,045
What are you doing? What?

542
00:26:29,046 --> 00:26:31,631
Listen, once you shoved that thing
in your pocket,

543
00:26:31,632 --> 00:26:33,425
you tainted chain of custody.

544
00:26:33,426 --> 00:26:35,969
If that badge leads to anything,
you'll never get a conviction.

545
00:26:35,970 --> 00:26:39,639
Federal government takes over a suicide.
CIA kidnaps us.

546
00:26:39,640 --> 00:26:41,850
The Reds try to kill us.

547
00:26:41,851 --> 00:26:44,145
Fuck the chain of custody.

548
00:26:45,062 --> 00:26:47,273
I want to find out
what my sister died over.

549
00:26:50,359 --> 00:26:52,361
[♪ Coco Montoya sings
"Lights Are On But Nobody's Home"]

550
00:26:54,864 --> 00:26:57,867
<i>♪ Playin with my mind, babe ♪</i>

551
00:27:01,871 --> 00:27:04,039
<i>♪ always accusin' me...</i>

552
00:27:04,040 --> 00:27:05,750
[groans]

553
00:27:07,043 --> 00:27:08,627
[knocking]

554
00:27:12,173 --> 00:27:14,341
<i>♪ I said,
you're playing with my mind ♪</i>

555
00:27:14,342 --> 00:27:15,760
- <i>♪ babe ♪</i>
- [knocking]

556
00:27:20,681 --> 00:27:22,725
<i>♪ always accusin' me ♪</i>

557
00:27:24,226 --> 00:27:26,394
- <i>♪ of doin' wrong...</i>
- New clothes.

558
00:27:26,395 --> 00:27:28,898
Guy was selling 'em outside the Way-Way
across the street.

559
00:27:30,649 --> 00:27:32,109
Wawa.

560
00:27:33,194 --> 00:27:34,944
How's Amisha holding up?

561
00:27:34,945 --> 00:27:37,530
She and the guards are in for the night.

562
00:27:37,531 --> 00:27:39,867
No injuries. They got away clean.

563
00:27:40,826 --> 00:27:43,703
Um, she's scared.

564
00:27:43,704 --> 00:27:45,497
People are trying to hurt her again.

565
00:27:45,498 --> 00:27:48,042
It's bringing back a lot of bad memories.

566
00:27:48,542 --> 00:27:50,002
<i>♪ Yeah ♪</i>

567
00:27:51,754 --> 00:27:53,880
<i>♪ Now here come my mother-in-law ♪</i>

568
00:27:53,881 --> 00:27:55,383
I travel light.

569
00:27:57,343 --> 00:27:59,010
<i>♪ It's the same thing every day...</i>

570
00:27:59,011 --> 00:28:00,429
You're hurt pretty bad.

571
00:28:02,014 --> 00:28:03,432
Been better.

572
00:28:04,475 --> 00:28:05,767
Been worse.

573
00:28:05,768 --> 00:28:06,811
Thank you.

574
00:28:07,895 --> 00:28:09,230
For giving me a second chance.

575
00:28:11,440 --> 00:28:13,567
Sorry I lied to you. I didn't want to.

576
00:28:14,652 --> 00:28:16,278
Yes, you did.

577
00:28:17,279 --> 00:28:18,947
That's why you said
a specific set of words

578
00:28:18,948 --> 00:28:20,950
in a specific way at a specific time.

579
00:28:21,909 --> 00:28:23,119
To tell a lie.

580
00:28:24,203 --> 00:28:25,703
But I've got a firm policy
to forgive liars

581
00:28:25,704 --> 00:28:28,207
who save me
from getting brained in an alley.

582
00:28:29,166 --> 00:28:30,625
<i>♪ I can see your lights...</i>

583
00:28:30,626 --> 00:28:32,086
Thank you.

584
00:28:33,921 --> 00:28:37,215
But I still wish
I didn't have to be deceptive.

585
00:28:37,216 --> 00:28:40,093
Getting to the truth,
getting the story out,

586
00:28:40,094 --> 00:28:42,471
it just required I not tell the truth.

587
00:28:43,889 --> 00:28:45,473
That was the cost.

588
00:28:45,474 --> 00:28:47,016
But is the cost worth it?

589
00:28:47,017 --> 00:28:51,438
We could've been killed back there
at that bowling alley or at the hotel.

590
00:28:51,439 --> 00:28:53,190
I don't run away from danger.

591
00:28:55,234 --> 00:28:56,818
You don't seem to, either.

592
00:28:56,819 --> 00:28:59,237
I'm a 250-pound former soldier.

593
00:28:59,238 --> 00:29:02,782
You're a 110-pound reporter,
and your security's average at best.

594
00:29:02,783 --> 00:29:04,784
Maybe you should take Amisha
and go back home.

595
00:29:04,785 --> 00:29:06,035
Never.

596
00:29:06,036 --> 00:29:10,081
What we're trying to do here,
it's bigger than just me and Amisha.

597
00:29:10,082 --> 00:29:11,291
And she agrees.

598
00:29:11,292 --> 00:29:13,168
We came here for information,
and the last person

599
00:29:13,169 --> 00:29:15,129
to have that information was in this city.

600
00:29:16,630 --> 00:29:18,298
So, no.

601
00:29:18,299 --> 00:29:19,591
We're not leaving.

602
00:29:19,592 --> 00:29:21,509
Even if you die as a result?

603
00:29:21,510 --> 00:29:24,763
I told you, I'm not a person who runs.

604
00:29:26,182 --> 00:29:27,892
What happened there?

605
00:29:30,144 --> 00:29:32,855
Guess I'm not a person who runs, either.

606
00:29:37,443 --> 00:29:39,153
Is that why you're still here?

607
00:29:44,700 --> 00:29:47,411
You feel bad
you couldn't help Anna, don't you?

608
00:29:48,496 --> 00:29:50,747
That poor woman looked you in the eyes,

609
00:29:50,748 --> 00:29:55,043
confused, petrified, broken,

610
00:29:55,044 --> 00:29:56,545
and you couldn't do anything.

611
00:29:59,340 --> 00:30:00,924
But hey,

612
00:30:00,925 --> 00:30:03,344
who do you think's more responsible?

613
00:30:05,054 --> 00:30:08,098
The man who said a few words
to Anna on a subway...

614
00:30:10,059 --> 00:30:12,019
...or the reporter who put her there?

615
00:30:17,816 --> 00:30:19,443
I'll get your back.

616
00:30:27,451 --> 00:30:31,205
My mother gave me this bracelet
when I was 16.

617
00:30:34,583 --> 00:30:37,753
She was a real journalist.

618
00:30:40,089 --> 00:30:42,341
Died in action in Indonesia.

619
00:30:46,178 --> 00:30:48,472
She's why I'm a reporter.

620
00:30:52,810 --> 00:30:54,603
This bracelet...

621
00:30:56,272 --> 00:30:58,440
...is all I have of her.

622
00:31:00,901 --> 00:31:02,735
Two seeds

623
00:31:02,736 --> 00:31:05,238
in each box,

624
00:31:05,239 --> 00:31:07,824
supposed to be...

625
00:31:07,825 --> 00:31:09,451
me and her...

626
00:31:11,954 --> 00:31:13,622
...always together.

627
00:31:16,667 --> 00:31:19,336
Each box a different stage of our lives.

628
00:31:23,549 --> 00:31:25,091
<i>♪ Accusin' me...</i>

629
00:31:25,092 --> 00:31:27,886
And I tell people
it's just a regular charm bracelet.

630
00:31:31,557 --> 00:31:33,309
For some reason...

631
00:31:35,394 --> 00:31:37,855
...it doesn't feel right
to lie to you again.

632
00:31:40,316 --> 00:31:43,027
<i>♪ Oh, but I can't see ♪</i>

633
00:31:44,111 --> 00:31:47,573
<i>♪ I can't see nobody home ♪</i>

634
00:31:48,741 --> 00:31:52,411
<i>♪ No, no, no ♪</i>

635
00:32:12,848 --> 00:32:15,643
Can't say I'm comfortable
doing this on my laptop.

636
00:32:16,644 --> 00:32:18,186
- I don't give a fuck.
- [chuckles]

637
00:32:18,187 --> 00:32:19,271
Yeah? Well, I do.

638
00:32:20,272 --> 00:32:22,523
Look, man, I may record CIA sites,

639
00:32:22,524 --> 00:32:24,984
use burners, live off the grid,
but all that's legal.

640
00:32:24,985 --> 00:32:27,445
Taking your sister's badge
from a crime scene?

641
00:32:27,446 --> 00:32:28,906
That's federal evidence tampering.

642
00:32:30,574 --> 00:32:32,283
Then leave.

643
00:32:32,284 --> 00:32:34,452
- You don't have to be here.
- [chuckles]

644
00:32:34,453 --> 00:32:37,039
Okay, yeah.
That's easy for you to say.

645
00:32:39,333 --> 00:32:42,669
You want to know why
I only write for online publications now?

646
00:32:42,670 --> 00:32:46,089
Hmm? Because, a year ago, I was working
a banking scandal that implicated

647
00:32:46,090 --> 00:32:49,008
the Fed and a lot of the powers that be,
including my own newspaper,

648
00:32:49,009 --> 00:32:52,262
who I discovered had been sitting
on the story for years.

649
00:32:53,597 --> 00:32:56,057
When the watchers essentially work
for the watched,

650
00:32:56,058 --> 00:32:58,017
yeah, you don't stand a chance.

651
00:32:58,018 --> 00:33:02,397
They not only fired me,
they discredited me, humiliated me,

652
00:33:02,398 --> 00:33:04,565
called me a crackpot,

653
00:33:04,566 --> 00:33:07,443
planted insane conspiracy theory documents
on my laptop,

654
00:33:07,444 --> 00:33:10,114
manipulated my expense reports,
make it look like that I was stealing.

655
00:33:11,115 --> 00:33:12,449
Made my own father doubt me.

656
00:33:14,993 --> 00:33:16,328
So, yeah...

657
00:33:17,996 --> 00:33:19,665
...I need to be here.

658
00:33:21,792 --> 00:33:24,210
Here. Knew we had one in evidence.

659
00:33:24,211 --> 00:33:27,380
Fraud raided a fake ID ring
a few weeks ago. It should work.

660
00:33:27,381 --> 00:33:28,631
Okay.

661
00:33:28,632 --> 00:33:29,758
[laptop beeps]

662
00:33:30,676 --> 00:33:31,801
Ready to go.

663
00:33:31,802 --> 00:33:34,638
[tense, dramatic music playing]

664
00:33:38,142 --> 00:33:39,767
[beeping]

665
00:33:39,768 --> 00:33:41,853
Let's see what we got here.

666
00:33:41,854 --> 00:33:44,021
- [beep]
- All right.

667
00:33:44,022 --> 00:33:46,566
Name: Anna Merrick.

668
00:33:46,567 --> 00:33:49,485
Work address and access allowance

669
00:33:49,486 --> 00:33:51,488
to Building 28, station J12.

670
00:33:53,907 --> 00:33:55,451
Level Eight clearance?

671
00:33:56,535 --> 00:33:58,202
Thought you said
your sister was an HR clerk?

672
00:33:58,203 --> 00:33:59,997
What the hell's she doing
with Level Eight clearance?

673
00:34:02,124 --> 00:34:04,083
I-I have no idea.

674
00:34:04,084 --> 00:34:06,587
Well, at least we know
where we're going to next.

675
00:34:07,629 --> 00:34:09,839
Tell me that badge isn't from the car.

676
00:34:09,840 --> 00:34:12,383
Forget what you think you see and leave.

677
00:34:12,384 --> 00:34:14,720
Well, I see stolen evidence.

678
00:34:16,638 --> 00:34:18,432
No. You don't.

679
00:34:19,641 --> 00:34:21,268
You're gonna intimidate me?

680
00:34:22,144 --> 00:34:24,104
I'm no fuckin' rat, Green.

681
00:34:25,105 --> 00:34:26,647
I'm no idiot.

682
00:34:26,648 --> 00:34:28,274
This is a federal case.

683
00:34:28,275 --> 00:34:32,738
I'm not you. I'm not content
to blow up my career over bullshit.

684
00:34:33,614 --> 00:34:35,574
So if they come to me, if they ask me...

685
00:34:37,326 --> 00:34:39,286
...I'm gonna tell 'em what I've seen.

686
00:34:42,080 --> 00:34:44,082
Go back to your desk, Shaun.

687
00:34:49,922 --> 00:34:52,341
All right, grab your shit. We got to move.

688
00:34:56,053 --> 00:34:58,764
You know you're way too big
to make a quiet getaway, right?

689
00:34:59,765 --> 00:35:00,973
Didn't want to wake you.

690
00:35:00,974 --> 00:35:04,685
Don't always sneak out in the middle of
the night, but I have a drive ahead of me.

691
00:35:04,686 --> 00:35:06,479
Where to?

692
00:35:06,480 --> 00:35:07,773
Baltimore.

693
00:35:08,607 --> 00:35:10,691
Want to get there
first thing in the morning.

694
00:35:10,692 --> 00:35:12,527
Gives me two and a half hours tops.

695
00:35:12,528 --> 00:35:14,028
What's there?

696
00:35:14,029 --> 00:35:15,780
Sampson.

697
00:35:15,781 --> 00:35:18,200
After what you told me,
there's more we need to discuss.

698
00:35:20,035 --> 00:35:22,120
Just come back safe, okay?

699
00:35:25,123 --> 00:35:27,960
I don't want anyone else
I dragged into this getting hurt.

700
00:35:32,714 --> 00:35:34,007
[entry bell chimes]

701
00:35:37,261 --> 00:35:39,263
[slow, dramatic music playing]

702
00:35:45,853 --> 00:35:47,938
What the hell was Anna doing here?

703
00:35:52,150 --> 00:35:54,235
[breathing heavily]

704
00:35:54,236 --> 00:35:56,238
♪ ♪

705
00:36:01,285 --> 00:36:03,287
[indistinct crowd chatter]

706
00:36:05,831 --> 00:36:07,499
[Reacher] Springfield.

707
00:36:09,001 --> 00:36:10,669
Here we go.

708
00:36:14,756 --> 00:36:18,927
Reacher. Congressman Sampson was wondering
when he'd see you again.

709
00:36:19,845 --> 00:36:20,929
Follow me.

710
00:36:25,100 --> 00:36:26,810
[elevator bell dings]

711
00:36:33,525 --> 00:36:35,151
- You coming?
- Last time

712
00:36:35,152 --> 00:36:38,196
you said I was going to meet Sampson,
I wound up talking to federal flunkies.

713
00:36:39,031 --> 00:36:40,908
How do I know
you're not gonna screw me again?

714
00:36:41,992 --> 00:36:43,535
Get on the elevator and find out.

715
00:36:50,334 --> 00:36:52,085
[elevator bell dings]

716
00:36:54,046 --> 00:36:57,965
Oh. Unless you want to be
bouncing off the walls, stop at half.

717
00:36:57,966 --> 00:37:00,010
Half it is.

718
00:37:01,011 --> 00:37:02,553
[knocking at door]

719
00:37:02,554 --> 00:37:04,722
Come in, Springfield.

720
00:37:04,723 --> 00:37:06,682
Shouldn't leave the door open.

721
00:37:06,683 --> 00:37:09,227
You called me from the elevator
20 seconds ago.

722
00:37:10,228 --> 00:37:11,729
Reacher. [clears throat]

723
00:37:11,730 --> 00:37:12,981
Good to see you again.

724
00:37:13,982 --> 00:37:15,650
Would you like breakfast?

725
00:37:15,651 --> 00:37:17,568
I never turn down a meal.

726
00:37:17,569 --> 00:37:18,737
Then sit.

727
00:37:20,739 --> 00:37:22,531
There's a poached egg and a cup of fruit.

728
00:37:22,532 --> 00:37:23,659
I haven't touched it.

729
00:37:25,243 --> 00:37:26,912
[Reacher] Thank you.

730
00:37:28,246 --> 00:37:30,331
I can only assume that you came down here

731
00:37:30,332 --> 00:37:32,959
because you feel
there's information you want,

732
00:37:32,960 --> 00:37:35,128
or you have information to share.

733
00:37:36,129 --> 00:37:38,005
Which one is it?

734
00:37:38,006 --> 00:37:39,799
Both.

735
00:37:39,800 --> 00:37:41,176
[Sampson] Then go ahead.

736
00:37:44,554 --> 00:37:47,975
I already told you.
We have no secrets between us.

737
00:37:50,018 --> 00:37:51,812
All right.

738
00:37:52,813 --> 00:37:55,606
I want to know about your involvement
with General Putra.

739
00:37:55,607 --> 00:37:59,235
He tortured and slaughtered a number
of political dissidents in Indonesia.

740
00:37:59,236 --> 00:38:02,154
Mm-hmm. And?

741
00:38:02,155 --> 00:38:04,992
And my source tells me that he was trained
by Delta Force, led by you.

742
00:38:06,868 --> 00:38:08,244
And by the way you're squirming,

743
00:38:08,245 --> 00:38:10,204
I'd say you know the general's name,
and you were involved

744
00:38:10,205 --> 00:38:11,581
- in his atrocities.
- John...

745
00:38:12,791 --> 00:38:14,250
You're awfully calm
for a man being accused

746
00:38:14,251 --> 00:38:15,751
of what could be war crimes.

747
00:38:15,752 --> 00:38:17,878
[John] Your source only got part
of the story right.

748
00:38:17,879 --> 00:38:21,132
Yes, I was in Indonesia
with Delta Force in 1998,

749
00:38:21,133 --> 00:38:22,800
and I met Putra.

750
00:38:22,801 --> 00:38:24,552
I didn't much like him.

751
00:38:24,553 --> 00:38:26,971
Our mission was top secret,
and it remains so.

752
00:38:26,972 --> 00:38:28,723
But what I can disclose is that

753
00:38:28,724 --> 00:38:31,768
I have never tortured or killed civilians.

754
00:38:32,894 --> 00:38:36,565
And I've never assisted
in torturing or killing civilians.

755
00:38:37,691 --> 00:38:40,735
If you're trying to get a rise out of me,
it won't work.

756
00:38:40,736 --> 00:38:42,695
Simply because I'm telling the truth.

757
00:38:42,696 --> 00:38:44,947
Why didn't you tell me all this
the first time we met?

758
00:38:44,948 --> 00:38:49,035
Do you typically offer up details
of your top secret military operations

759
00:38:49,036 --> 00:38:51,455
- when a stranger asks?
- Point taken.

760
00:38:53,165 --> 00:38:55,291
Is there anything you can tell me
that you're not?

761
00:38:55,292 --> 00:38:56,417
Nothing.

762
00:38:56,418 --> 00:38:57,835
What about your chief of staff?

763
00:38:57,836 --> 00:38:59,503
- Cahill?
- What about him?

764
00:38:59,504 --> 00:39:00,921
The night Anna Merrick killed herself,

765
00:39:00,922 --> 00:39:03,925
there were four witnesses on the train,
but only three were interviewed by police.

766
00:39:04,718 --> 00:39:07,136
One ran away. It was Cahill.

767
00:39:07,137 --> 00:39:08,138
[scoffs]

768
00:39:09,973 --> 00:39:11,766
Excuse my language, but that's bullshit.

769
00:39:11,767 --> 00:39:13,934
- I saw him.
- Cahill wasn't on that train.

770
00:39:13,935 --> 00:39:15,436
- He would've told me.
- [Reacher] Would he?

771
00:39:15,437 --> 00:39:17,980
Seems people have a habit
of leaving out important details

772
00:39:17,981 --> 00:39:19,190
from the discussion around here.

773
00:39:19,191 --> 00:39:21,067
- Not Nolan Cahill.
- [Reacher] Really?

774
00:39:21,068 --> 00:39:23,903
Where is he? We could go ask him together.

775
00:39:23,904 --> 00:39:25,696
Fine.

776
00:39:25,697 --> 00:39:26,907
Let's go.

777
00:39:28,325 --> 00:39:30,327
[birds singing]

778
00:39:31,369 --> 00:39:32,871
[Sampson] Nolan.

779
00:39:34,790 --> 00:39:36,416
We need to speak.

780
00:39:39,586 --> 00:39:41,712
[exhales]

781
00:39:41,713 --> 00:39:43,839
Things never stay hidden, do they?

782
00:39:43,840 --> 00:39:46,217
What does that mean?

783
00:39:46,218 --> 00:39:50,138
Watergate, Iran-Contra,
Hunter's fucking laptop.

784
00:39:50,972 --> 00:39:53,557
Whatever people try to hide,
it always comes out.

785
00:39:53,558 --> 00:39:56,352
Why were you on the subway
with Anna Merrick the night she died?

786
00:39:56,353 --> 00:39:58,771
[dramatic music playing]

787
00:39:58,772 --> 00:40:00,565
Did you take her jump drive?

788
00:40:03,026 --> 00:40:04,903
You would've made a good president.

789
00:40:07,739 --> 00:40:09,281
I'm sorry, John.

790
00:40:09,282 --> 00:40:10,784
[Sampson] Nolan.

791
00:40:11,868 --> 00:40:12,869
- Nolan. Nolan!
- No!

792
00:40:15,205 --> 00:40:16,580
[car alarm ringing]

793
00:40:16,581 --> 00:40:17,999
Oh, my God.

794
00:40:19,084 --> 00:40:21,795
This is Springfield.
I need a perimeter and paramedic.

795
00:40:29,427 --> 00:40:31,680
[♪ Pixies play "Where's My Mind"]

796
00:40:34,766 --> 00:40:38,603
<i>♪ With your feet on the air
and your head on the ground ♪</i>

797
00:40:42,023 --> 00:40:45,359
<i>♪ try this trick and spin it ♪</i>

798
